{"name":"Satellite","hex":"#9f8d89","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"satellite","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An attendant on an important person; a member of someone's retinue, often in a somewhat derogatory sense; a henchman. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A moon or other smaller body orbiting a larger one."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To transmit by satellite.","To orbit, like a satellite"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/satellite"}],"entries":[{"title":"Satellite","blocks":[{"text":"A satellite or an artificial satellite is an object, typically a spacecraft, placed into orbit around a celestial body. They have a variety of uses, including communication relay, weather forecasting, navigation (GPS), broadcasting, scientific research, and Earth observation. Additional military uses are reconnaissance, early warning, signals intelligence and, potentially, weapon delivery. Other satellites include the final rocket stages that place satellites in orbit and formerly useful satellites that later become defunct."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Two_3U_CubeSats.jpg/330px-Two_3U_CubeSats.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satellite","wikidata":"Q26540"},{"title":"Satellite (P.O.D. album)","blocks":[{"text":"Satellite is the fourth studio album by Christian nu metal band P.O.D. It was released by Atlantic Records on September 11, 2001. It was the band's last album with guitarist Marcos Curiel until When Angels and Serpents Dance (2008). According to drummer and backing vocalist Wuv Bernardo, the album is more mature than the band's previous work, and has a unique sound, combining heavy metal with elements of hip-hop and reggae. This, along with the Christian lyrics, made it stand out from other nu metal albums, many of which had themes of misery, pain and sadness."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/f3/POD-Satellite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satellite_(P.O.D._album)","wikidata":"Q666741"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Sputnik_asm.jpg/500px-Sputnik_asm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Sputnik_asm.jpg/500px-Sputnik_asm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Replica of the Sputnik 1","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASputnik_asm.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Ion_Engine_Test_Firing_-_GPN-2000-000482.jpg/500px-Ion_Engine_Test_Firing_-_GPN-2000-000482.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Ion_Engine_Test_Firing_-_GPN-2000-000482.jpg/1280px-Ion_Engine_Test_Firing_-_GPN-2000-000482.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Ion_Engine_Test_Firing_-_GPN-2000-000482.jpg/500px-Ion_Engine_Test_Firing_-_GPN-2000-000482.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Firing of Deep Space 1's ion thruster","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIon_Engine_Test_Firing_-_GPN-2000-000482.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/ISS-54_ELC-1%2C_main_solar_arrays_and_radiators_seen_from_the_Cupola.jpg/500px-ISS-54_ELC-1%2C_main_solar_arrays_and_radiators_seen_from_the_Cupola.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/ISS-54_ELC-1%2C_main_solar_arrays_and_radiators_seen_from_the_Cupola.jpg/1280px-ISS-54_ELC-1%2C_main_solar_arrays_and_radiators_seen_from